<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walvis Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walvis Bay is a port town on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, the only good natural harbour on that desert shore. In the 19th century it was the outlet for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gun trade of the interior, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the bay in 1878, later attaching it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. It remained a South African enclave after Namibian independence in 1990 and was transferred to Namibia in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walvis-bay.jpg" alt="Walvis Bay" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian McMorrow · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walvis_Bay_aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jonker's mounted gunmen raided Herero cattle and controlled trade routes to <b>Walvis Bay</b>, where cattle were exchanged for guns, horses and brandy.</blockquote><a href="/state/orlam-afrikaner-state">Orlam Afrikaner State</a></li></ul></section>
